.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/ansi-styles Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "ansi-styles [![Build Status](https://travis-ci.org/chalk/ansi-styles.svg?branch=master)](https://travis-ci.org/chalk/ansi-styles)"
.PP
.RS 4
\fRANSI escape codes\fP for styling strings in the terminal 
.RE
.PP

.PP
You probably want the higher-level \fRchalk\fP module for styling your strings\&.

.PP
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install ansi\-styles
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
const style = require('ansi\-styles');

console\&.log(`${style\&.green\&.open}Hello world!${style\&.green\&.close}`);


// Color conversion between 16/256/truecolor
// NOTE: If conversion goes to 16 colors or 256 colors, the original color
//       may be degraded to fit that color palette\&. This means terminals
//       that do not support 16 million colors will best\-match the
//       original color\&.
console\&.log(style\&.bgColor\&.ansi\&.hsl(120, 80, 72) + 'Hello world!' + style\&.bgColor\&.close);
console\&.log(style\&.color\&.ansi256\&.rgb(199, 20, 250) + 'Hello world!' + style\&.color\&.close);
console\&.log(style\&.color\&.ansi16m\&.hex('#abcdef') + 'Hello world!' + style\&.color\&.close);
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
Each style has an \fRopen\fP and \fRclose\fP property\&.
