.SH "Advanced usage"
.PP
By default, you get a map of styles, but the styles are also available as groups\&. They are non-enumerable so they don't show up unless you access them explicitly\&. This makes it easier to expose only a subset in a higher-level module\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstyle\&.modifier\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstyle\&.color\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstyle\&.bgColor\fP
.PP
.SS "Example"
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(style\&.color\&.green\&.open);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Raw escape codes (i\&.e\&. without the CSI escape prefix \fR\\u001B[\fP and render mode postfix \fRm\fP) are available under \fRstyle\&.codes\fP, which returns a \fRMap\fP with the open codes as keys and close codes as values\&.
.SS "Example"
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(style\&.codes\&.get(36));
//=> 39
.fi
.PP
.SH "\fR256 / 16 million (TrueColor) support\fP"
.PP
\fRansi-styles\fP uses the \fR\fRcolor-convert\fP\fP package to allow for converting between various colors and ANSI escapes, with support for 256 and 16 million colors\&.

.PP
The following color spaces from \fRcolor-convert\fP are supported:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRrgb\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRhex\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRkeyword\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRhsl\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRhsv\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRhwb\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRansi\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRansi256\fP
.PP

.PP
To use these, call the associated conversion function with the intended output, for example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
style\&.color\&.ansi\&.rgb(100, 200, 15); // RGB to 16 color ansi foreground code
style\&.bgColor\&.ansi\&.rgb(100, 200, 15); // RGB to 16 color ansi background code

style\&.color\&.ansi256\&.hsl(120, 100, 60); // HSL to 256 color ansi foreground code
style\&.bgColor\&.ansi256\&.hsl(120, 100, 60); // HSL to 256 color ansi foreground code

style\&.color\&.ansi16m\&.hex('#C0FFEE'); // Hex (RGB) to 16 million color foreground code
style\&.bgColor\&.ansi16m\&.hex('#C0FFEE'); // Hex (RGB) to 16 million color background code
.fi
.PP
